# Final Project Report: Olist Delivery Delay Prediction

## 1. Problem Definition

Late deliveries are one of the clearest drivers of poor customer experience in e-commerce. On the Olist platform, orders sometimes arrive after their estimated delivery date, and there is currently no way to flag this risk before it happens. This project aimed to build a model that predicts, at the moment an order is placed, whether it is likely to be delivered late. A working early-warning system would let a business proactively manage customer expectations or intervene with sellers/carriers before a delay actually damages the customer relationship. This matters because a missed, unflagged late delivery is a worse outcome than a false alarm — the cost of the two error types is not symmetric.

## 2. Data Preparation

The dataset used was the Brazilian E-Commerce Public Dataset by Olist, consisting of 9 linked tables covering orders, order items, products, customers, sellers, payments, reviews, and geolocation. Data preparation followed three stages. First, missing values were handled on a column-by-column basis rather than with a single blanket rule: some gaps (like missing delivery dates for cancelled orders) were deliberately left as flagged nulls rather than imputed, since fabricating a delivery date for an order that was never delivered would be misleading; other gaps (like missing review comments) were filled with constants since the absence itself was meaningful. Second, data quality issues were addressed: exact duplicate rows (found only in the geolocation table, 261,831 rows) were removed, and inconsistent city name spellings were cleaned using text normalization plus fuzzy matching. Third, features were engineered specifically to avoid data leakage — an initial version of the model included a feature derived from the actual delivery date, which was caught and removed because it would not be available at real prediction time. The final feature set (price, freight value, and promised delivery days) was validated to contain no information derived from the outcome being predicted. The data was split into training and test sets using a stratified 80/20 split, with the test set held out and untouched until final evaluation.

## 3. Model Development

Two baseline classification models were trained on the same training data: Logistic Regression and Random Forest. Logistic Regression performed poorly in a specific and informative way — it predicted "on time" for every single order in the test set, achieving 0 precision and 0 recall on the "late" class. This is a direct consequence of class imbalance (most orders in the dataset are on time) combined with a feature set that has limited linear separability for this problem. Random Forest performed meaningfully better, though still weak in absolute terms: it achieved a recall of 0.1494 and precision of 0.4241 on the "late" class, with an overall accuracy of 0.9322 (a number that is misleadingly high given how rarely the model actually flags a late order).

## 4. Model Tuning

Hyperparameter tuning was performed on the Random Forest model using grid search with 3-fold cross-validation, optimizing for F1 score rather than accuracy given the class imbalance. The search space covered `n_estimators` (100, 200), `max_depth` (5, 10, None), and `min_samples_leaf` (1, 5, 10). The best configuration found was `{max_depth: None, min_samples_leaf: 1, n_estimators: 100}`, with a cross-validation F1 score of 0.189. Notably, this matches the parameters the model was already using by default before tuning, meaning the search did not find an improvement within the space searched. This suggests either that the current feature set is the limiting factor rather than model configuration, or that a broader hyperparameter search (or entirely different model family) would be needed to meaningfully improve results.

## 5. Diagnostics

Three diagnostic checks were run on the tuned model. The learning curve showed training F1 scores remaining high (roughly 0.90–0.98) while validation F1 scores stayed consistently low (roughly 0.07–0.19) across all training set sizes tested, with the gap between the two not meaningfully closing as more data was added — a clear sign of overfitting rather than a data-volume problem. The calibration curve showed the model's predicted probabilities are overconfident: in the mid-to-high probability range, the model's stated confidence (60–80%) consistently overstated the true observed rate of lateness (closer to 40–45%). This means the model's raw probability outputs should not be trusted at face value for any downstream decision requiring calibrated risk estimates. A confusion-matrix-based error analysis on the test set additionally revealed that false negatives (missed late deliveries) heavily outnumber true positives (1,241 vs. 218), and that the model's misses skew toward higher-priced orders specifically (missed late orders averaged $144.70 in price versus $86.49 for correctly caught ones) — meaning the model underperforms exactly where the cost of a miss is likely to be highest.

## 6. Final Results

On the held-out test set, the final selected model (Random Forest) achieved: accuracy 0.9322, precision 0.4241, recall 0.1494, F1-score 0.2210, ROC-AUC 0.6628, PR-AUC 0.2138, and Brier score 0.0616. The confusion matrix showed 20,908 true negatives, 296 false positives, 1,241 false negatives, and 218 true positives. The low recall and F1 score indicate the model, while better than random guessing (ROC-AUC above 0.5), is not yet reliable enough to serve as a production early-warning system without further improvement.

## 7. Model Interpretation

Feature importance for the Random Forest model showed price (0.476) and freight_value (0.411) as by far the dominant drivers of predictions, with promised_days contributing comparatively little (0.113). This pattern is worth treating with caution: promised_days would logically be expected to carry strong predictive signal for a delivery-timing outcome, and its low importance alongside price's dominance — combined with the error analysis finding that the model specifically underperforms on higher-priced orders — suggests price may be functioning as an indirect proxy for some unmeasured factor (such as product type, shipping distance, or seller characteristics) rather than being a robust standalone predictor of lateness.

## 8. Production Readiness

The final trained model was serialized using `joblib.dump()` to `final_model.joblib`, and reloading was explicitly tested by loading the artifact fresh and re-running the full evaluation pipeline against the untouched test set, confirming the saved model reproduces identical results. A simple inference workflow was built and tested against 8 synthetic new/unseen input examples, demonstrating the model can load, accept new tabular input, generate prediction probabilities, apply the classification threshold, and return final binary predictions — without repeating any preprocessing logic outside the model itself, since the three input features require no transformation before being passed to the model.

## 9. Limitations & Future Improvements

The model's core limitation is its low recall on the "late" class, meaning it currently misses the majority of orders that will actually be late — the exact failure mode most costly to the business. This is compounded by clear overfitting (seen in the learning curve) and poor probability calibration, both of which limit how much the current model can be trusted in production as-is. The most promising path forward is expanding the feature set: seller-to-customer geographic distance, seller-level historical on-time delivery rate, and seasonal or carrier-specific indicators were not available in this iteration but are likely to carry much stronger predictive signal than price and freight value alone. Additionally, given the cost asymmetry between false negatives and false positives, future work should explore lowering the classification threshold below 0.5 to trade some precision for meaningfully higher recall, and should consider class-balancing techniques (such as class weighting or resampling) during training, since the current model's behavior is consistent with it defaulting toward the majority class under imbalance. Finally, a wider hyperparameter search space and alternative model families (e.g. gradient boosting) are worth testing, since the current tuning converged on the untouched default configuration without improvement.